{"name":"Lady Luck","hex":"#47613c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Lady Luck","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female personification of luck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Lady_Luck"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fortuna","blocks":[{"text":"Fortuna, sometimes anglicized as Fortune, is the goddess of luck or fortune in Roman religion. She came to represent life's capriciousness, and was a goddess of fate. In antiquity she was also known by the epithet Automatia. Her Greek equivalent is Tyche."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Fortuna%2C_inv._2244_-_Braccio_Nuovo%2C_Museo_Chiaramonti_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC00920.jpg/330px-Fortuna%2C_inv._2244_-_Braccio_Nuovo%2C_Museo_Chiaramonti_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC00920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortuna","wikidata":"Q4654"},{"title":"Tyche","blocks":[{"text":"Tyche was the presiding tutelary deity who governed the fortune and prosperity of a city, its destiny. In Classical Greek mythology, she is the daughter of the Titans Tethys and Oceanus, and she brings positive messages to people relating to external events outside their control."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Istanbul_-_Museo_archeol._-_Tyche_e_Plutone_-_sec._II_d.C._-_Foto_G._Dall%27Orto_28-5-2006.jpg/330px-Istanbul_-_Museo_archeol._-_Tyche_e_Plutone_-_sec._II_d.C._-_Foto_G._Dall%27Orto_28-5-2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyche","wikidata":"Q213440"},{"title":"Lady Luck (comics)","blocks":[{"text":"Lady Luck is an American comic-strip and comic-book crime fighter and adventuress created and designed in 1940 by Will Eisner with artist Chuck Mazoujian. She starred in a namesake, four-page weekly feature published in a Sunday newspaper comics insert colloquially called \"The Spirit Section\", which ran from June 2, 1940, to November 3, 1946. Her adventures were reprinted in comic books published by Quality Comics. A revamped version of the character debuted in 2013 in DC Comics's Phantom Stranger comic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f5/Lady_Luck_%28comics_character%29.jpg/330px-Lady_Luck_%28comics_character%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Luck_(comics)","wikidata":"Q6470362"},{"title":"Lady","blocks":[{"text":"A lady is a woman who behaves in a polite way. Once used to describe only women of a high social class or status, the female counterpart of lord, now it may refer to any adult woman, as gentleman can be used for men."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady","wikidata":"Q1378024"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/CarminaBurana_wheel.jpg/500px-CarminaBurana_wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/CarminaBurana_wheel.jpg/1280px-CarminaBurana_wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/CarminaBurana_wheel.jpg/500px-CarminaBurana_wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fortuna governs the circle of the four stages of life, the Wheel of Fortune, in a manuscript of Carmina Burana[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarminaBurana_wheel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Antoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png/500px-Antoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Antoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png/1280px-Antoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Antoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png/500px-Antoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fortuna on the reverse of a Roman coin of the Emperor Aurelian.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntoninianus_-_Avrelianus%2C_FORTVNA_REDVX.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/HumiliationValerianusHolbein.jpg/500px-HumiliationValerianusHolbein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/HumiliationValerianusHolbein.jpg/500px-HumiliationValerianusHolbein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The humiliation of Emperor Valerian by king Shapur I of Persia (260) passed into European cultural memory as an instance of the reversals of Fortuna. In Hans Holbein's pen-and-ink drawing (1521), the universal lesson is brought home by its contemporary setting.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHumiliationValerianusHolbein.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Allegory_of_Fortune_mg_0010.jpg/500px-Allegory_of_Fortune_mg_0010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Allegory_of_Fortune_mg_0010.jpg/1280px-Allegory_of_Fortune_mg_0010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Allegory_of_Fortune_mg_0010.jpg/500px-Allegory_of_Fortune_mg_0010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fortuna lightly balances the orb of sovereignty between thumb and finger in a Dutch painting of ca 1530 (Musé